---

<BaseLayout title={`${item.title} — Copy-Ready Prompt & Beat Breakdown | AI Rap Video`} description={item.description} canonicalPath={`/prompts/${item.slug}/`}>
  <JsonLd data={[articleLd, breadcrumbLd]} />
  <article class="prose hero">
    <p class="tag">{item.intent}</p>
    <h1>{item.title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">The flagship AI rap video prompt: two separate portraits become a left/right duo performance — traded bars, fixed sides, a hanging mic between them, and a clean 8-12 second loop. Every other template in this wiki is a variation of this structure. It also doubles as the free answer to every "rap duo video generator" search: the prompt plus your own model replaces the credit meter entirely.</p>

    <h2>Use this as your free rap duo video generator</h2>
    <p>Trend-generator sites charge credits to produce this exact clip on their locked stage. The same output needs only: two prepped photos, this prompt pasted whole, and one render in any image-to-video model you already use — no subscription, no upload queue, no watermark. The <a href="/ai-rap-video-generator/">generator comparison page</a> breaks down all three routes side by side; this template is the prompt layer of that method.</p>

    <h2>When to use this template</h2>
    <p>Use it when you have two people, two clear portrait photos, and permission from both — a friendship, a partnership, a birthday duo, a "we actually did it" post. If both photos are of you, switch to the <a href="/prompts/ai-rap-video-self-duo-two-outfits/">self-duo template</a>; if the energy is romantic rather than hype, the <a href="/prompts/ai-rap-video-couple-prompt/">couple template</a> reframes it.</p>

    <h2>Hook &amp; verse structure</h2>
    <p>The duo format lives on <strong>call-and-response</strong>. Before prompting, write four short lines: the left performer owns lines 1 and 3 (the setup), the right performer owns lines 2 and 4 (the answer). That alternation is what makes the clip feel like a real trade instead of two people talking over each other. Keep each line under 10 syllables — image-to-video models hold lip-sync best on short phrases, and the <a href="/prompts/ai-rap-video-original-lyrics-prompt/">original lyrics template</a> generates them to fit.</p>

    <h2>Copy-ready main prompt</h2>
    <pre class="prompt-box">Create an AI rap video duo performance from two separate portrait references.
Left performer = photo 1, right performer = photo 2.
Keep both faces recognizable, front-lit, and assigned to fixed sides for the whole clip — the left performer never crosses to the right, and vice versa.
Stage: original studio booth, warm orange key light, a single hanging microphone centered between them, dark background with soft rim light.
Wardrobe: keep each performer's outfit exactly as shown in their reference photo.
Action: energetic head nods on the beat, traded bars with clear mouth movement per line, confident posture, occasional shoulder lean toward the mic.
Camera: locked medium two-shot framing both from the waist up, slow push-in during the final two seconds.
Audio: original upbeat rap cadence, call-and-response — left performer leads, right performer answers. Write NEW original lyrics; never quote commercial songs.
Duration: 8-12 seconds, ending on a clean two-shot hold.
Template focus: AI Rap Video Duo Prompt (Two Photos).</pre>

    <h2>Beat &amp; camera breakdown</h2>
    <ol class="list">
      <li><strong>0-2s — Establish.</strong> Locked two-shot. Both faces lit, the hanging mic reads clearly between them. No movement except a shared head nod on the first beat.</li>
      <li><strong>2-5s — Left leads.</strong> Line 1 and part of line 2 land on the left performer: mouth movement, forward lean, right performer nods along silently.</li>
      <li><strong>5-9s — Right answers.</strong> The trade reverses. Right performer takes the answering lines with the same energy; left drops back to nodding.</li>
      <li><strong>9-12s — Push-in hold.</strong> Slow push toward the mic, both performers land a final shared pose. The hold is the loop point.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Negative prompt</h2>
    <pre class="prompt-box">identity swap, faces changing sides, merged faces, third person appearing, extra limbs, deformed hands, blurry faces, sunglasses, masks, group photo input, on-screen lyrics or karaoke text, watermarks, brand logos, celebrity lookalike, NSFW, camera shake, scene changes</pre>

    <h2>Photo prep for this template</h2>
    <ul class="list">
      <li>One face per photo — no pairs, no group crops split into two uploads.</li>
      <li>Both photos shot from a similar angle and distance so the two performers read at the same scale.</li>
      <li>Distinct outfits or colors; two near-identical looks invite side swaps.</li>
      <li>The full <a href="/prompts/ai-rap-video-photo-prep-checklist/">photo prep checklist</a> covers crops, glasses, and lighting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Render steps (any model)</h2>
    <ol class="list">
      <li>Upload photo 1 and photo 2 into your model's two reference slots, in that order — the prompt's left/right assignment follows slot order.</li>
      <li>Paste the main prompt. If your tool has a separate negative field, put the negative block there; otherwise append it after "Avoid:" in the same prompt.</li>
      <li>Set duration 8-12s and aspect 9:16 (or 16:9 for YouTube), then render.</li>
      <li>Review the full clip against the beat breakdown. If sides drift, apply the fixes in the <a href="/prompts/ai-rap-video-troubleshooting-faces/">face troubleshooting guide</a> — usually one wording change, not a rewrite.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Common failure modes</h2>
    <ul class="list">
      <li><strong>Faces swap sides mid-clip</strong> → restate "fixed sides for the whole clip" and make the outfits more distinct. Full fix list in the <a href="/prompts/ai-rap-video-troubleshooting-faces/">troubleshooting guide</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>One face goes soft</strong> → that performer's photo is lower quality or smaller in frame; re-crop closer and brighter.</li>
      <li><strong>Both mouths move on every line</strong> → the call-and-response roles are too vague; name which performer owns which lines explicitly in the Action block.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>Can I use three or more performers?</h3>
    <p>Two is the reliable ceiling for most image-to-video models — each extra face multiplies identity drift. For groups, render two-person pairs and cut them together.</p>
    <h3>Which performer should lead?</h3>
    <p>Whoever has the sharper photo. The lead gets more close framing and more mouth movement, so their reference does more work.</p>

    <div class="notice"><strong>Compliance:</strong> original staging and lyrics only. Do not commercially bind protected recordings — including the Hotel Lobby trend audio — or celebrity likenesses. Get consent from everyone in the photos.</div>

    <VideoGallery videos={videosFor(item.slug)} heading="See it in action" sub="Real videos from the web built in this exact format." viewAll={true} />

    <RelatedPrompts slugs={item.related} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
